@@ -0,0 +1,92 @@
+# CSV clip-manifest preview
+
+A coach validates an exported clip manifest before importing it elsewhere. The preview is read-only: it never saves clips, never changes project data, and never reads or processes video. There is no import or save action.
+
+## File format
+
+The file is UTF-8. A leading BOM is optional and is stripped. Line endings may be LF or CRLF. A header row is required. Column names are matched case-insensitively after trimming, so `Tag Type` and `tag type` are the same column.
+
+Quoting follows [RFC 4180](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180). A field that contains a comma, a double quote, or a newline is wrapped in double quotes, and an embedded double quote is written twice (`""`).
+
+Columns that are not in the table below are ignored: their values are not validated and do not appear on the clip. They still count toward the header width. A data row whose field count differs from the header is malformed (`wrong number of columns`).
+
+A blank line — no fields, or every field empty — is ignored. It is not listed in `rows` and it is not counted in `summary.total`. Line numbers still count physical lines. The header is line 1, and a data row's `line` is the physical line where that record starts. A quoted field that spans several lines is one row, numbered at its first line.
+
+## Columns
+
+| Column | Required | Rule |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `Tag Type` | yes | must name an existing project tag type (exact, after trim) |
+| `Start (s)` | yes | finite number, `>= 0` |
+| `End (s)` | yes | finite number, `> Start (s)` |
+| `Label` | no | free text, displayed escaped |
+| `Notes` | no | free text |
+| `Players` | no | `;`-separated names or `#number`; each must match exactly one project player |
+| `Clip ID` | no | if present, must not equal an existing clip id or an earlier row's id |
+| `Duration (s)` | no | ignored (the app's own export writes it) |
+
+`Tag Type`, `Start (s)`, and `End (s)` must appear in the header. Missing one of those is a request error, not a row error. The other five columns may be absent.
+
+Player tokens are split on `;` and trimmed; an empty token is dropped. A name matches one project player case-insensitively. A token that starts with `#` matches that player's number, including the export form `#7 Alice` (the text after `#` up to the first space) and a bare `#7`. No match is `unknown player: <token>`. More than one match is `ambiguous player: <token>`. A missing or empty Players cell means no players.
+
+## Row statuses
+
+Each counted row has one status: `valid`, `malformed`, or `duplicate`. `malformed` wins over `duplicate`.
+
+`malformed` means a column rule failed, the row has the wrong number of columns, or a cell is undecodable. A file that is not UTF-8 never reaches row status; that is the request error below. `duplicate` means the row itself is well formed, but it repeats the Tag Type, Start (s), End (s), and Label of an earlier comparable row, or its Clip ID is already used by a clip on the project or by an earlier row.
+
+Clip ID is reserved from any earlier physical row regardless of that row's status. A later well-formed row that repeats it is `duplicate`. The (Tag Type, Start, End, Label) key is reserved from earlier well-formed rows, including rows already flagged duplicate, and from a malformed row whose start and end are still finite numbers. An unknown tag, a player error, or an end that is not after the start does not drop the key. The key is not reserved when the column count is wrong or either time is not a finite number, so two such rows are not duplicates of each other.
+
+Reason strings, as emitted:
+
+- `unknown tag type`
+- `start must be a finite number >= 0`
+- `end must be a finite number`
+- `end must be greater than start`
+- `wrong number of columns`
+- `unknown player: <token>`
+- `ambiguous player: <token>`
+- `clip id already exists`
+- `same clip id as line N`
+- `same as line N`
+
+A malformed row may list more than one column reason. A duplicate row lists exactly one, checked in this order: an id already on a project clip, then an id used on an earlier line, then the matching key. `N` is that earlier row's physical line.
+
+## Contract
+
+`POST /api/projects/<project_id>/clips/import_preview`
+
+Multipart form with one field, `file`, holding the CSV. The handler loads the project and returns JSON. It never writes `projects.json`, never creates a clip, and never reads a video file.
+
+Success is `200`:
+
+```json
+{"preview_only": true,
+ "summary": {"total": 3, "valid": 1, "malformed": 1, "duplicate": 1},
+ "rows": [
+   {"line": 2, "status": "valid", "reasons": [],
+    "clip": {"tag_type": "Pass", "start": 1.5, "end": 4.0, "label": "x",
+             "notes": "", "players": ["<player id>"]}},
+   {"line": 3, "status": "malformed", "reasons": ["end must be greater than start"], "clip": null},
+   {"line": 4, "status": "duplicate", "reasons": ["same as line 2"], "clip": null}]}
+```
+
+`preview_only` is always true. `summary.total` counts data rows only (`valid` + `malformed` + `duplicate`), not physical lines and not blank lines. `clip.players` holds project player ids. `clip` is null when the status is `malformed` or `duplicate`.
+
+A missing project is `404 {"error": "Project not found"}` with no `code`.
+
+`400 {"error": "...", "code": "bad_request"}` covers three cases:
+
+- missing `file`: `{"error": "No file provided", "code": "bad_request"}`
+- bytes that are not UTF-8: `{"error": "File could not be decoded as UTF-8", "code": "bad_request"}`
+- header missing `Tag Type`, `Start (s)`, or `End (s)`: `{"error": "Missing required header", "code": "bad_request"}`
+
+Above 5000 data rows the response is `400 {"error": "Too many rows", "code": "too_many_rows"}`. Blank lines do not count toward that limit. The body is the error, not a truncated preview.
+
+## Using it in the tagging screen
+
+Press `M`, or activate the **Import preview** button (`#btn-import-preview`, accessible name "Import preview"), to open the file picker (`#import-preview-file`). The hint beside the button reads `M import preview`, and the button title is `Preview a CSV clip manifest (M)`. `M` does nothing while a modal is open or while focus is in a text field. The same file can be selected again: the input is cleared after each choice so the change event fires.
+
+While the file is checked, the `role="status"` region shows `Checking…`, and the previous summary and table rows are cleared. `#import-preview-table` then has one `tr[data-status]` per counted CSV row, with columns for Line, Status, Tag type, Start, End, Label, Players, and Reasons. Displayed text, including Label, is escaped so markup in the file stays text. Notes are not shown in the table; they remain available only as `clip.notes` in the JSON response. `#import-preview-summary` reads `Total N · valid N · malformed N · duplicate N`. An empty manifest sets the status to `No rows found`. A network failure sets it to `Preview failed`. Any other failure shows the server's `error` text, such as `Too many rows` or `Missing required header`.
+
+**Close**, or Escape while focus is inside the results, dismisses them and returns focus to the Import preview button. Switching projects discards the panel and drops a response that is still in flight. The row scroller and Close are reachable and operable from the keyboard. Nothing here writes the manifest back to the project.
